// §4.5.15, third bullet, verbatim: "Analog operators are not allowed in the
// repeat, while and non-genvar for looping statements." §5.9 states the same
// restriction from the statement side: "The following restrictions are applied
// to looping statements (repeat, while and for) except for analog_for
// statements: - Analog filter functions are not allowed".
//
// "non-genvar" is the whole of the exception: §5.9.3's analog_for unrolls onto
// the spine, so an operator inside one is evaluated every iteration. There is
// no analysis_or_constant carve-out here — §4.5.15 grants one to
// `if`/`case`/`?:` in its FIRST bullet only. (The event-body ban is a third,
// separate bullet — 88_operator_in_event.va.)
//
// This fixture used to assert the opposite — that the illegal form COMPILES.
//! lrm 4.5.15
//! reject E0514
// §5.9: "Analog filter functions are not allowed" in repeat/while/non-genvar
// for — `integer i` makes this a procedural for, not a §5.9.3 analog_for.
// Rejected as E0514.
module ch4_op_for(p); inout p; electrical p; integer i; real x; analog begin for(i=0;i<2;i=i+1) x=ddt(V(p)); I(p)<+x; end endmodule
